An unchanged document update repeatedly scanned pointer identities, existing UUIDs and prior outputs. Replace those scans with standard-library identity sets and a first-existing-UUID index while preserving caller order, duplicate-UUID first-match behavior, object identity and ordered timestamp/event requests.

The same frozen release benchmark improves the 10,000-document unchanged workload from 55.109 ms to 0.897 ms median (61.46× over five samples); 1k/5k medians also improve. A 2,048-document mixed regression covers repeated pointers, pointer-distinct equal UUIDs, first-existing selection, reverse incoming order, separate rebuilds and exact effect positions. All 16 tests, formatting and scoped all-target Clippy pass offline/locked. Exact runners, source snapshots and raw before/after outputs are retained, including the original passing-tests/failed-test-style capture.

No manifest/dependency change or database/UI/native Java runtime is introduced. Timings qualify this Rust workload and host, not universal tiny-input performance or native SysON speed. Cargo-build RSS is not claimed as planner-only memory. Depends on F25 #24; land that prerequisite first, retarget to integration, inspect and revalidate rather than merging into its feature branch. Code/regression/benchmark and generated evidence/documentation are separate commits. Independent coordinator source review found no blocking issue within this scope.
